# G4's list for the gem5 host (C.GEM5_SMOKE_LIST), and the list that
# "--stage baseline" records for gem5 unless --baseline-list says otherwise.
# Two smoke-size workloads from two different programs.
#
# G4 asks one question: with the feature ON, does the host finish cleanly?
# It is not a score and not a direction check. So the right list is the
# cheapest pair that still runs two different kinds of code. Each entry is
# a few million guest instructions (approx_insts in hosts/gem5/workloads.json
# holds the qemu count). At the O3 speed the brief assumes, 100 K to 300 K
# instructions per second, each run takes well under a minute. So a broken
# port fails G4 in about a minute. TODO(measured): the lead records the real
# gem5 time after the first cluster run.
#
# gapbs_bfs_s  a compact C++ graph kernel. Its branches follow the graph
#              data, and it reads its input from data/gapbs/, so it also
#              covers the run dir's data path.
# sqlite_s     the largest code in the set: a SQL parser, a planner, B-trees
#              and a bytecode engine. A port meets many distinct branches
#              here, which is where a table-indexing mistake shows up.
#
# Both programs check their own result and exit 1 on a mismatch, so a port
# that corrupts execution fails here too, not only one that crashes.
gapbs_bfs_s
sqlite_s
